Problem:
Let \[f(x) =
\begin{cases}
3x + 5 &\text{if }x<-3, \\
7-4x&\text{if }x\ge -3.
\end{cases}
\]Find $f(-10)$.

Solution:
answer:  To find \( f(-10) \), we need to determine which piece of the piecewise function to use. The function \( f(x) \) is defined as:

\[
f(x) =
\begin{cases}
3x + 5 & \text{if } x < -3, \\
7 - 4x & \text{if } x \ge -3.
\end{cases}
\]

Since \( -10 \) is less than \( -3 \), we use the first piece of the function:

\[
f(-10) = 3(-10) + 5
\]

Now, we perform the calculation:

\[
f(-10) = 3(-10) + 5 = -30 + 5 = -25
\]

Therefore, the value of \( f(-10) \) is \(\boxed{-25}\).
