Problem:
If $p(t)$ and $q(t)$ are both seventh-degree polynomials in $t$, what is the degree of $p(t)\cdot q(t)$?

Solution:
answer:  To determine the degree of the product \( p(t) \cdot q(t) \), we need to consider the degrees of the individual polynomials \( p(t) \) and \( q(t) \).

Given:
- \( p(t) \) is a seventh-degree polynomial.
- \( q(t) \) is a seventh-degree polynomial.

When two polynomials are multiplied, the degree of the resulting polynomial is the sum of the degrees of the individual polynomials. Therefore, the degree of \( p(t) \cdot q(t) \) is:

\[
\text{Degree of } p(t) \cdot q(t) = \text{Degree of } p(t) + \text{Degree of } q(t)
\]

Since both \( p(t) \) and \( q(t) \) are seventh-degree polynomials:

\[
\text{Degree of } p(t) \cdot q(t) = 7 + 7 = 14
\]

Thus, the degree of \( p(t) \cdot q(t) \) is:

\[
\boxed{14}
\]

Final Answer: The final answer is \( \boxed{14}\). I hope it is correct.
