Problem:
You have a square that has a side length of $1$ centimeter. Each minute, $2$ centimeters are added to the side length. After $10$ minutes, what will be the difference between the area of the last square and the area of the initial square in square centimeters (do not include the units in your answer)?

Solution:
answer:  The side length of the square increases by $2$ centimeters each minute. Therefore, after $10$ minutes, the side length will be:

\[ 1 + 2 \times 10 = 21 \text{ centimeters} \]

The area of a square is given by the square of its side length. So, the area of the square after $10$ minutes is:

\[ 21^2 = 441 \text{ square centimeters} \]

The initial side length of the square is $1$ centimeter, so the area of the initial square is:

\[ 1^2 = 1 \text{ square centimeters} \]

The difference between the area of the last square and the area of the initial square is:

\[ 441 - 1 = 440 \text{ square centimeters} \]

Therefore, the difference between the area of the last square and the area of the initial square after $10$ minutes is:

\[ \boxed{440} \]

Final Answer: The final answer is \(440\). I hope it is correct.
====================

