Problem:
In the diagram, $K$, $O$ and $M$ are the centers of the three semi-circles. Also, $OC = 32$ and $CB = 36$.

[asy]
pair A, K, O, C, M, B, X, Y, Z, J, T;
O=(0,0);
C=(32,0);
M=(50,0);
B=(68,0);
A=(-68,0);
K=(A+C)/2;
X=(0,68);
Y=(-18,50);
Z=(50,18);
J=(7,43.3);
T=(59,15.6);
path nom, bigc, middlec, smallc;
nom=A--B--(100,100)--(-100,100)--cycle;
bigc=A..X..B--cycle;
middlec=A..Y..C--cycle;
smallc=C..Z..B--cycle;
fill(bigc, gray(.5));
fill(middlec, white);
fill(smallc, white);
draw(smallc);
draw(middlec);
draw(bigc);
draw(A--B);
label("A", A, S);
label("K", K, S);
label("O", O, S);
label("M", M, S);
label("C", C, S);
label("B", B, S);
label("S", J, SW);
label("E", T, SW);
label("$l$", (.9(J-T)+J), NW);
draw((.9(J-T)+J)--(.5(T-J)+T));
dot(K);
dot(O);
dot(M);
dot(J);
dot(T);
[/asy] Line $l$ is drawn to touch the smaller semi-circles at points $S$ and $E$ so that $KS$ and $ME$ are both perpendicular to $l$.  Determine the area of quadrilateral $KSEM$.

Solution:
answer:  Since $KS$ and $ME$ are perpendicular to $l$, points $S$ and $E$ are the centers of the smaller semi-circles. Therefore, $KS$ and $ME$ are the radii of the smaller semi-circles.

The area of a semi-circle with radius $r$ is $\frac{1}{2}\pi r^2$. Therefore, the area of the larger semi-circle with radius $OC = 32$ is $\frac{1}{2}\pi (32)^2 = 512\pi$.

The area of the smaller semi-circle with radius $CB = 36$ is $\frac{1}{2}\pi (36)^2 = 648\pi$.

The area of quadrilateral $KSEM$ is the difference between the area of the larger semi-circle and the area of the smaller semi-circles. Therefore, the area of quadrilateral $KSEM$ is $512\pi - 256\pi - 144\pi = \boxed{112\pi}$.

Final Answer: The final answer is $112\pi$. I hope it is correct.
