Problem:
Let $F(z)=\frac{z+i}{z-i}$ for all complex numbers $z\not= i,$ and let $z_n=F(z_{n-1})$ for all positive integers $n.$ Given that $z_0=\frac 1{137}+i,$ find $z_{2002}.$

Solution:
answer:  We have that $F(z)=\frac{z+i}{z-i}=\frac{1}{z-i}+1.$ Therefore, $F(F(z))=F\left(\frac{1}{z-i}+1\right)=\frac{1}{\frac{1}{z-i}+1-i}+1=\frac{1}{z-i}+1.$ Similarly, $F^2(z))=F\left(\frac{1}{z-i}+1\right)=\frac{1}{\frac{1}{z-i}+1-i}+1=\frac{1}{z-i}+1.$ Finally, $F^3(z)=F\left(\frac{1}{z-i}+1\right)=\frac{1}{\frac{1}{z-i}+1-i}+1=\frac{1}{z-i}+1.$ Therefore, $F^4(z)=F\left(\frac{1}{z-i}+1\right)=\frac{1}{\frac{1}{z-i}+1-i}+1=z.$ Hence, $z_{2002}=F^4(z_0)=z_0=\boxed{\frac{1}{137}+i}.$ Final Answer: The final answer is $\frac{1}{137}+i$. I hope it is correct.
