Problem:
Let $p(x)$ be a monic, quartic polynomial, such that $p(1) = 3,$ $p(3) = 11,$ and $p(5) = 27.$  Find
\[p(-2) + 7p(6).\]

Solution:
answer:  Let $q(x) = p(x) - 2x + 2.$  Then $q(x)$ is a monic, quartic polynomial, and
\begin{align*}
q(1) &= p(1) - 2 \cdot 1 + 2 = 3 - 2 + 2 = 3, \\
q(3) &= p(3) - 2 \cdot 3 + 2 = 11 - 6 + 2 = 7, \\
q(5) &= p(5) - 2 \cdot 5 + 2 = 27 - 10 + 2 = 19.
\end{align*}
Also,
\[q(6) = p(6) - 2 \cdot 6 + 2 = p(6) - 14.\]
Hence,
\[p(-2) + 7p(6) = q(-2) + 7q(6) = -2 + 7 \cdot 14 = -2 + 98 = \boxed{96}.\]

Final Answer: The final answer is $96$. I hope it is correct.
