Problem:
Let $f(x) = \frac{2x+9}{x-7}.$ Find the product of all solutions to the equation $f(f(x)) = x.$

Solution:
answer:  We have that $f(f(x)) = f\left(\frac{2x+9}{x-7}\right) = \frac{2\left(\frac{2x+9}{x-7}\right) + 9}{\frac{2x+9}{x-7} - 7} = \frac{\frac{4x+18 + 9(x-7)}{x-7}}{\frac{2x+9 - 7(x-7)}{x-7}} = \frac{4x+18 + 9x - 63}{2x+9 - 7x + 49} = \frac{13x - 45}{-5x + 50} = -\frac{13x - 45}{5x - 50}.$
The equation $f(f(x)) = x$ becomes $-\frac{13x - 45}{5x - 50} = x.$
Solving this equation, we find that the solutions are $x = 3$ and $x = 9.$
The product of these solutions is $3\cdot9 = \boxed{27}.$The answer is: 27
