Problem:
The ellipse shown below is defined by the equation
\[PF_1 + PF_2 = d.\]Find $d.$

[asy]
unitsize(0.3 cm);

int i, n = 10;

for (i = -n; i <= n; ++i) {
  draw((i,-n)--(i,n),gray(0.7));
  draw((-n,i)--(n,i),gray(0.7));
}

draw((0,-n)--(0,n));
draw((-n,0)--(n,0));

draw(shift((-1,0))*xscale(7)*yscale(5)*Circle((0,0),1),red);

dot((-1,0));
[/asy]

Solution:
answer:  The equation of the ellipse is given by \(PF_1 + PF_2 = d\), where \(P\) is any point on the ellipse and \(F_1\) and \(F_2\) are the foci. The distance between the foci \(F_1\) and \(F_2\) is \(2c\), where \(c\) is the distance from the center of the ellipse to each focus.

From the diagram, we can see that the center of the ellipse is at the origin \((0,0)\) and the foci are located at \((-1,0)\) and \((1,0)\). The distance between the foci is \(2\), so \(2c = 2\) and \(c = 1\).

The length of the major axis is \(2a\), where \(a\) is the distance from the center of the ellipse to any point on the major axis. From the diagram, we can see that the length of the major axis is \(6\), so \(2a = 6\) and \(a = 3\).

Therefore, \(d = 2a = 2\cdot3 = \boxed{6}\).
