Problem:
The parabola $y = ax^2 + bx + c$ crosses the $x$-axis at $(p,0)$ and $(q,0),$ both to the right of the origin.  A circle also passes through these two points.  Let $t$ be the length of the tangent from the origin to the circle.  Express $t^2$ in terms of one or more of the coefficients $a,$ $b,$ and $c.$

[asy]
unitsize(3 cm);

pair A, O, T;

real func (real x) {
  return ((x - 1)*(x - 2));
}

A = (1.5,-0.4);
O = (0,0);
T = intersectionpoint(Circle(A,abs(A - (1,0))),arc(A/2,abs(A)/2,0,90));

draw(graph(func,0.5,2.5));
draw((-0.5,0)--(2.5,0));
draw((0,-1)--(0,1));
draw(Circle(A,abs(A - (1,0))));
draw(O--T);

label("$t$", T/3, N);

dot(T);
[/asy]

Solution:
answer:  Since the parabola crosses the $x$-axis at $(p,0)$ and $(q,0)$, we can write the equation of the parabola as $y = a(x-p)(x-q)$.

The circle also passes through these two points, so its center is the midpoint of the segment joining these points, which is $\left(\frac{p+q}{2}, 0\right)$.

The radius of the circle is the distance from the center to either of the points, which is $\frac{|p-q|}{2}$.

The length of the tangent from the origin to the circle is the distance from the origin to the point on the circle, which is $t$.

Using the Pythagorean theorem, we have $t^2 = \left(\frac{p+q}{2}\right)^2 + \left(\frac{|p-q|}{2}\right)^2$.

Simplifying, we get $t^2 = \frac{(p+q)^2}{4} + \frac{(p-q)^2}{4} = \frac{(p+q)^2 + (p-q)^2}{4} = \boxed{\frac{2p^2+2pq+2q^2}{4}}$.
====================

