Problem:
What is the product (in base 10) of the first and last digits of the base-6 representation of $682_{10}$?

Solution:
answer:  First, we need to convert $682_{10}$ to base-6. We do this by repeatedly dividing the number by 6 and recording the remainders.

\[
682 \div 6 = 113 \quad \text{remainder} \quad 4
\]
\[
113 \div 6 = 18 \quad \text{remainder} \quad 5
\]
\[
18 \div 6 = 3 \quad \text{remainder} \quad 0
\]
\[
3 \div 6 = 0 \quad \text{remainder} \quad 3
\]

Reading the remainders from bottom to top, we get the base-6 representation of $682_{10}$ as $3054_6$.

The first digit of $3054_6$ is $3$ and the last digit is $4$.

The product of the first and last digits is:

\[
3 \times 4 = \boxed{12}
\]

Final Answer: $\boxed{12}$. I hope it is correct.
