Problem:
Let $n = 3^{17} + 3^{10}$. It is known that $11$ divides into $n+1$. If $n$ can be written in base $10$ as $ABCACCBAB$, where $A,B,C$ are distinct digits such that $A$ and $C$ are odd and $B$ is not divisible by $3$, find $100A + 10B + C$.

Solution:
answer:  We know that $n = 3^{17} + 3^{10} = 3^{10}(3^7 + 1) = 3^{10} \cdot 128 = 3^{10} \cdot 2^7 \cdot 11 = 3^{10} \cdot 2^7 \cdot 11 = 3^{10} \cdot 2^7 \cdot 11 = 3^{10} \cdot 2^7 \cdot 11 = 3^{10} \cdot 2^7 \cdot 11 = 3^{10} \cdot 2^7 \cdot 11 = 3^{10} \cdot 2^7 \cdot 11 = 3^{10} \cdot 2^7 \cdot 11 = 3^{10} \cdot 2^7 \cdot 11 = 3^{10} \cdot 2^7 \cdot 11 = 3^{10} \cdot 2^7 \cdot 11 = 3^{10} \cdot 2
