Problem:
What is the greatest common divisor of all of the members of the set containing all numbers that are the product of four consecutive positive integers?

Solution:
answer:  Let the four consecutive positive integers be \( n, n+1, n+2, \) and \( n+3 \). The product of these four integers is:

\[ n(n+1)(n+2)(n+3) \]

We need to find the greatest common divisor (GCD) of all such products. Notice that the product of any four consecutive integers will always include the factors 2, 3, and 5. This is because:

1. Among any four consecutive integers, there will be at least one multiple of 2.
2. Among any four consecutive integers, there will be at least one multiple of 3.
3. Among any four consecutive integers, there will be at least one multiple of 5.

Therefore, the product of any four consecutive integers will be divisible by \(2 \times 3 \times 5 = 30\).

Thus, the greatest common divisor of all the members of the set containing all numbers that are the product of four consecutive positive integers is:

\[ \boxed{30} \]

Final Answer: The final answer is $ \boxed{30} $. I hope it is correct.
====================

====================
