Problem:
The length of the year on the planet Mars is exactly 697 days. If Mars has a calendar with a 12-day week, and year 0 begins on the first day of the week, what is the next year which will begin on the first day of the week?

Solution:
answer:  To determine the next year which will begin on the first day of the week on Mars, we need to consider the length of the Martian year and the length of the Martian week.

1. The length of the Martian year is 697 days.
2. The length of the Martian week is 12 days.

First, we need to find out how many weeks are in a Martian year. We do this by dividing the length of the Martian year by the length of the Martian week:

\[
\frac{697 \text{ days}}{12 \text{ days/week}} = 58.0833 \text{ weeks}
\]

This means that there are 58 full weeks in a Martian year, and 1 extra day. Since year 0 begins on the first day of the week, the next year which will begin on the first day of the week will be:

\[
0 \text{ year} + 1 \text{ year} = 1 \text{ year}
\]

Therefore, the next year which will begin on the first day of the week is year:

\[
\boxed{1}
\]
