Problem:
Determine the smallest non-negative integer $a$ that satisfies the congruences: \begin{align*}
&a\equiv 2\pmod 3,\\
&a\equiv 4\pmod 5,\\
&a\equiv 6\pmod 7,\\
&a\equiv 8\pmod 9.
\end{align*}

Solution:
answer:  We can use the Chinese Remainder Theorem to solve this system of congruences. First, we find the least common multiple of the moduli, which is $3\cdot5\cdot7\cdot9=945$. Then, we find the inverse of each modulus modulo 945. The inverse of 3 is 35, the inverse of 5 is 19, the inverse of 7 is 11, and the inverse of 9 is 13. Now, we multiply each congruence by the corresponding inverse:

\begin{align*}
&a\equiv 2\cdot 35\pmod{945},\\
&a\equiv 4\cdot 19\pmod{945},\\
&a\equiv 6\cdot 11\pmod{945},\\
&a\equiv 8\cdot 13\pmod{945}.
\end{align*}

Simplifying each congruence, we get:

\begin{align*}
&a\equiv 14\pmod{945}.
\end{align*}

Therefore, the smallest non-negative integer $a$ that satisfies all the given congruences is $a = \boxed{14}$.
