Problem:
Kathy needs money for her trip to Europe. If she has 300 US dollars in the bank but wants to withdraw half of it in British pounds and half of it in euros, how many more euros than pounds will she have? Assume 1 pound = 1.64 USD and 1 euro = 1.32 USD, and round to the nearest whole number.

Solution:
answer:  First, we need to determine how much money Kathy will withdraw in each currency. Since she wants to withdraw half of her 300 US dollars in each currency, we calculate:

\[
\frac{300}{2} = 150 \text{ USD}
\]

Next, we convert 150 USD to British pounds using the given exchange rate:

\[
\frac{150 \text{ USD}}{1.64 \text{ USD/pound}} \approx 91.67 \text{ pounds}
\]

Then, we convert 150 USD to euros using the given exchange rate:

\[
\frac{150 \text{ USD}}{1.32 \text{ USD/euro}} \approx 118.33 \text{ euros}
\]

Now, we find the difference between the number of euros and pounds:

\[
118.33 \text{ euros} - 91.67 \text{ pounds} \approx 27 \text{ euros}
\]

Therefore, Kathy will have \boxed{27} more euros than pounds.
====================

