Problem:
A robot moving forward at a constant speed takes 2.5 hours to travel 1 kilometer. Moving forward at this same constant speed, it takes the robot 90 seconds to travel the length of a particular hallway. How many meters long is the hallway?

Solution:
answer:  First, we need to determine the speed of the robot. The robot takes 2.5 hours to travel 1 kilometer. Therefore, the speed is:

\[
\text{Speed} = \frac{1 \text{ kilometer}}{2.5 \text{ hours}} = \frac{1}{2.5} \text{ kilometers per hour} = 0.4 \text{ kilometers per hour}
\]

Next, we need to find the length of the hallway. The robot takes 90 seconds to travel the length of the hallway. Using the speed calculated:

\[
\text{Distance} = \text{Speed} \times \text{Time} = 0.4 \text{ kilometers per hour} \times \frac{90 \text{ seconds}}{3600 \text{ seconds per hour}} = 0.4 \text{ kilometers} \times \frac{1}{40} = 0.1 \text{ kilometers} = 0.1 \times 1000 = 100 \text{ meters}
\]

Therefore, the length of the hallway is \boxed{100} meters.
====================

