Problem:
Let $a,$ $b,$ $c,$ $p,$ $q,$ and $r$ be real numbers such that
\[\begin{vmatrix} p & b & c \\ a & q & c \\ a & b & r \end{vmatrix} = 0.\]Assuming that $a \neq p,$ $b \neq q,$ and $c \neq r,$ find the value of $\frac{p}{p - a} + \frac{q}{q - b} + \frac{r}{r - c}.$

Solution:
answer:  We have that
\[\begin{vmatrix} p & b & c \\ a & q & c \\ a & b & r \end{vmatrix} = 0.\]Expanding the determinant, we get
\[p(aq - bc) - b(ar - ac) + c(aq - ab) = 0.\]Rearranging, we get
\[p(aq - bc) + b(ar - ac) + c(aq - ab) = 0.\]Since $a \neq p,$ $b \neq q,$ and $c \neq r,$ we can divide both sides by $aq - bc$ to get
\[\frac{p}{p - a} + \frac{q}{q - b} + \frac{r}{r - c} = 0.\]Therefore, the value of $\frac{p}{p - a} + \frac{q}{q - b} + \frac{r}{r - c}$ is $\boxed{0}.$The answer is: 0
