Problem:
When $\begin{pmatrix} a \\ b \end{pmatrix}$ is projected onto $\begin{pmatrix} \sqrt{3} \\ 1 \end{pmatrix},$ the resulting vector has magnitude $\sqrt{3}.$  Also, $a = 2 + b \sqrt{3}.$  Enter all possible values of $a,$ separated by commas.

Solution:
answer:  The projection of $\begin{pmatrix} a \\ b \end{pmatrix}$ onto $\begin{pmatrix} \sqrt{3} \\ 1 \end{pmatrix}$ is given by

$$\frac{\begin{pmatrix} a \\ b \end{pmatrix} \cdot \begin{pmatrix} \sqrt{3} \\ 1 \end{pmatrix}}{\begin{pmatrix} \sqrt{3} \\ 1 \end{pmatrix} \cdot \begin{pmatrix} \sqrt{3} \\ 1 \end{pmatrix}} \begin{pmatrix} \sqrt{3} \\ 1 \end{pmatrix}.$$

We are given that the magnitude of this projection is $\sqrt{3}$, so we have

$$\frac{\begin{pmatrix} a \\ b \end{pmatrix} \cdot \begin{pmatrix} \sqrt{3} \\ 1 \end{pmatrix}}{\begin{pmatrix} \sqrt{3} \\ 1 \end{pmatrix} \cdot \begin{pmatrix} \sqrt{3} \\ 1 \end{pmatrix}} = \sqrt{3}.$$

Simplifying, we get

$$\frac{a + b}{4} = \sqrt{3}.$$

Since $a = 2 + b\sqrt{3}$, we have $a = \boxed{2}$.
