Let $\mathbf{a} = \begin{pmatrix} 4 \\ \sqrt{\pi} \\ -14 \end{pmatrix}$ and $\mathbf{b} = \begin{pmatrix} -1 \\ 3 \\ 10 \end{pmatrix}.$  Compute $\mathbf{a} \cdot (\mathbf{a} \times \mathbf{b}).$

Solution:
answer:  First, we need to compute the cross product $\mathbf{a} \times \mathbf{b}$.

\[
\mathbf{a} \times \mathbf{b} = \begin{vmatrix}
\mathbf{i} & \mathbf{j} & \mathbf{k} \\
4 & \sqrt{\pi} & -14 \\
-1 & 3 & 10
\end{vmatrix}
= \begin{pmatrix} 40 \\ -12\sqrt{\pi} \\ -156 \end{pmatrix}
\]

Next, we compute the dot product $\mathbf{a} \cdot (\mathbf{a} \times \mathbf{b})$.

\[
\mathbf{a} \cdot (\mathbf{a} \times \mathbf{b}) = \begin{pmatrix} 4 \\ \sqrt{\pi} \\ -14 \end{pmatrix} \cdot \begin{pmatrix} 40 \\ -12\sqrt{\pi} \\ -156 \end{pmatrix}
= (4)(40) + (\sqrt{\pi})(-12\sqrt{\pi}) + (-14)(-156)
= 160 - 12\pi + 2184
= \boxed{2
