Problem:
Compute
\[\cos \frac{2 \pi}{7} \cos \frac{4 \pi}{7} \cos \frac{8 \pi}{7}.\]

Solution:
answer:  We can use the product-to-sum identities to simplify the expression. Recall that

\[\cos A \cos B \cos C = \frac{1}{8} \left[ \cos (A+B+C) + \cos (A-B+C) + \cos (A+B-C) \right].\]

In this case, we have

\[\cos \frac{2 \pi}{7} \cos \frac{4 \pi}{7} \cos \frac{8 \pi}{7} = \frac{1}{8} \left[ \cos \left( \frac{2 \pi}{7} + \frac{4 \pi}{7} + \frac{8 \pi}{7} \right) + \cos \left( \frac{2 \pi}{7} - \frac{4 \pi}{7} + \frac{8 \pi}{7} \right) + \cos \left( \frac{2 \pi}{7} + \frac{4 \pi}{7} - \frac{8 \pi}{7} \right) \right].\]

Simplifying the terms inside the brackets, we get

\[\boxed{0}.\]
