Problem:
The functions $\sin(x)$ and $\cos(x)$ are periodic with least period $2\pi$. What is the least period of the function $\cos(\sin(x))$?

Solution:
answer:  To determine the least period of the function \(\cos(\sin(x))\), we need to understand the behavior of the inner function \(\sin(x)\) and how it interacts with the outer function \(\cos(y)\).

The inner function \(\sin(x)\) has a period of \(2\pi\). This means that \(\sin(x + 2\pi) = \sin(x)\) for all \(x\).

The outer function \(\cos(y)\) also has a period of \(2\pi\). This means that \(\cos(y + 2\pi) = \cos(y)\) for all \(y\).

When we consider \(\cos(\sin(x))\), the inner function \(\sin(x)\) will complete one full cycle when \(x\) increases by \(2\pi\). Therefore, \(\sin(x)\) will cycle through its values every \(2\pi\).

Since \(\sin(x)\) completes one full cycle every \(2\pi\), the outer function \(\cos(\sin(x))\) will also complete one full cycle every \(2\pi\).

Thus, the least period of \(\cos(\sin(x))\) is \(2\pi\).

Solution: \(\boxed{2\pi}\)
