Problem:
Find the $2 \times 2$ matrix $\mathbf{M}$ such that $\mathbf{M} \begin{pmatrix} 2 \\ 7 \end{pmatrix} = \begin{pmatrix} -15 \\ -6 \end{pmatrix}$ and $\mathbf{M} \begin{pmatrix} 4 \\ -1 \end{pmatrix} = \begin{pmatrix} 15 \\ 18 \end{pmatrix}.$

Solution:
answer:  We can write the given equations as:
\begin{align*}
\mathbf{M} \begin{pmatrix} 2 \\ 7 \end{pmatrix} &= \begin{pmatrix} -15 \\ -6 \end{pmatrix}, \\
\mathbf{M} \begin{pmatrix} 4 \\ -1 \end{pmatrix} &= \begin{pmatrix} 15 \\ 18 \end{pmatrix}.
\end{align*}
This gives us the following system of equations:
\begin{align*}
2a + 7b &= -15, \\
4a - b &= 15.
\end{align*}
We can solve this system by adding the two equations together:
\begin{align*}
2a + 7b + 4a - b &= -15 + 15, \\
6a + 6b &= 0.
\end{align*}
This simplifies to:
\begin{align*}
a + b &= 0, \\
a &= -b.
\end{align*}
Therefore, the matrix $\mathbf{M}$ is:
\begin{align*}
\mathbf{M} &= \begin{pmatrix} -1 & 1 \\ 1 & -1 \end{pmatrix}.
\end{align*}
\boxed{-1}
