Problem:
If $\mathbf{A}^{-1} = \begin{pmatrix} 4 & 0 \\ 1 & -1 \end{pmatrix}$ and $\mathbf{B}^{-1} = \begin{pmatrix} 2 & -1 \\ -1 & 3 \end{pmatrix},$ then find the inverse of $\mathbf{A} \mathbf{B}.$

Solution:
answer:  We know that the inverse of the product of two matrices is the product of their inverses. Therefore, the inverse of $\mathbf{A} \mathbf{B}$ is given by:

\[
(\mathbf{A} \mathbf{B})^{-1} = \mathbf{A}^{-1} \mathbf{B}^{-1}
\]

Substituting the given matrices:

\[
(\mathbf{A} \mathbf{B})^{-1} = \begin{pmatrix} 4 & 0 \\ 1 & -1 \end{pmatrix} \begin{pmatrix} 2 & -1 \\ -1 & 3 \end{pmatrix}
\]

We perform the matrix multiplication:

\[
(\mathbf{A} \mathbf{B})^{-1} = \begin{pmatrix} 4 \cdot 2 + 0 \cdot (-1) & 4 \cdot (-1) + 0 \cdot 3 \\ 1 \cdot 2 + (-1) \cdot (-1) & 1 \cdot (-1) + (-1) \cdot 3 \end{pmatrix}
\]

\[
\Rightarrow \boxed{\begin{pmatrix} 8 & -4 \\ 3 & -4 \end{pmatrix}}
\]
====================

