Problem:
The matrix for reflecting through a certain plane $P,$ which passes through the origin, is given by
\[\renewcommand{\arraystretch}{1.5} \begin{pmatrix} \frac{11}{15} & \frac{2}{15} & \frac{2}{3} \\ \frac{2}{15} & \frac{14}{15} & -\frac{1}{3} \\ \frac{2}{3} & -\frac{1}{3} & -\frac{2}{3} \end{pmatrix} \renewcommand{\arraystretch}{1}.\]Find the normal vector of plane $P.$  Enter your answer in the form $\begin{pmatrix} a \\ b \\ c \end{pmatrix},$ where $a,$ $b,$ and $c$ are integers, $a > 0,$ and $\gcd(|a|,|b|,|c|) = 1.$

Solution:
answer:  The normal vector of the plane $P$ is the same as the normal vector of the reflection matrix. The normal vector of the reflection matrix is given by the columns of the matrix.

The columns of the given matrix are:
\[
\begin{pmatrix} \frac{11}{15} \\ \frac{2}{15} \\ \frac{2}{3} \end{pmatrix}, \quad \begin{pmatrix} \frac{2}{15} \\ \frac{14}{15} \\ -\frac{1}{3} \end{pmatrix}, \quad \begin{pmatrix} \frac{2}{3} \\ -\frac{1}{3} \\ -\frac{2}{3} \end{pmatrix}
\]

We can see that these columns are linearly independent, so they form a basis for the plane $P$. The normal vector of the plane $P$ is the first column of the matrix, which is:
\[
\begin{pmatrix} \frac{11}{15} \\ \frac{2}{15} \\ \frac{2}{3} \end{pmatrix}
\]

\boxed{\begin{pmatrix}11 \\ 2 \\ 2\end{pmatrix}}
