Problem:
Compute $\begin{pmatrix} -3 \\ 5 \end{pmatrix} \cdot \begin{pmatrix} -2 \\ 1 \end{pmatrix}$.

Solution:
answer:  To compute the dot product of the vectors \(\begin{pmatrix} -3 \\ 5 \end{pmatrix}\) and \(\begin{pmatrix} -2 \\ 1 \end{pmatrix}\), we use the formula for the dot product of two vectors \(\begin{pmatrix} a_1 \\ a_2 \end{pmatrix}\) and \(\begin{pmatrix} b_1 \\ b_2 \end{pmatrix}\):

\[
\begin{pmatrix} a_1 \\ a_2 \end{pmatrix} \cdot \begin{pmatrix} b_1 \\ b_2 \end{pmatrix} = a_1 b_1 + a_2 b_2
\]

Substituting the given vectors into the formula:

\[
\begin{pmatrix} -3 \\ 5 \end{pmatrix} \cdot \begin{pmatrix} -2 \\ 1 \end{pmatrix} = (-3)(-2) + (5)(1)
\]

Calculating each term:

\[
(-3)(-2) = 6
\]
\[
(5)(1) = 5
\]

Adding these results together:

\[
6 + 5 = 11
\]

\(\boxed{11}\)
