Problem:
The matrix for projecting onto a certain plane $P,$ which passes through the origin, is given by
\[\renewcommand{\arraystretch}{1.5} \begin{pmatrix} \frac{13}{14} & -\frac{1}{7} & \frac{3}{14} \\ -\frac{1}{7} & \frac{5}{7} & \frac{3}{7} \\ \frac{3}{14} & \frac{3}{7} & \frac{5}{14} \end{pmatrix} \renewcommand{\arraystretch}{1}.\]Find the normal vector of plane $P.$  Enter your answer in the form $\begin{pmatrix} a \\ b \\ c \end{pmatrix},$ where $a,$ $b,$ and $c$ are integers, $a > 0,$ and $\gcd(|a|,|b|,|c|) = 1.$

Solution:
answer:  The normal vector of the plane $P$ is the same as the first column of the projection matrix. Therefore, the first column of the given matrix is

\[\begin{pmatrix} \frac{13}{14} \\ -\frac{1}{7} \\ \frac{3}{14} \end{pmatrix}.\]

To express the normal vector in the form \(\begin{pmatrix} a \\ b \\ c \end{pmatrix}\) where \(a\), \(b\), and \(c\) are integers, \(a > 0\), and \(\gcd(|a|,|b|,|c|) = 1\), we multiply each component by 14 to clear the denominators:

\[\begin{pmatrix} 13 \\ -2 \\ 3 \end{pmatrix}.\]

Next, we find the greatest common divisor (gcd) of the absolute values of the components:

\[\gcd(|13|,|-2|,|3|) = \gcd(13, 2, 3) = 1.\]

Thus, the normal vector of plane $P$ is \(\boxed{\begin{pmatrix} 13 \\ -2 \\ 3 \end{pmatrix}}\).
