Problem:
Find the matrix $\mathbf{R}$ such that for any vector $\mathbf{v},$ $\mathbf{R} \mathbf{v}$ is the reflection of $\mathbf{v}$ through the $xy$-plane.

Solution:
answer:  To find the matrix \(\mathbf{R}\) that reflects a vector \(\mathbf{v}\) through the \(xy\)-plane, we need to understand the effect of this transformation on the vector.

A reflection through the \(xy\)-plane changes the \(z\)-coordinate of the vector while keeping the \(x\)- and \(y\)-coordinates unchanged. Specifically, if \(\mathbf{v} = \begin{pmatrix} x \\ y \\ z \end{pmatrix}\), then the reflected vector \(\mathbf{R} \mathbf{v}\) will be \(\begin{pmatrix} x \\ y \\ -z \end{pmatrix}\).

This transformation can be represented by a matrix \(\mathbf{R}\) such that:
\[
\mathbf{R} \begin{pmatrix} x \\ y \\ z \end{pmatrix} = \begin{pmatrix} x \\ y \\ -z \end{pmatrix}
\]

Thus, the matrix \(\mathbf{R}\) is:
\[
\mathbf{R} = \begin{pmatrix} 1 & 0 & 0 \\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\ 0 & 0 & -1 \end{pmatrix}
\]

The matrix \(\mathbf{R}\) is \(\boxed{\mathbf{R}}\).
